    "text": "How antibody is generated by B cell and matured?"
  },
  {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "B cells generate antibodies through V(D)J recombination, which randomly assembles immunoglobulin gene segments to create a unique B-cell receptor. When a B cell encounters its matching antigen and receives help from T cells, it activates, proliferates, and differentiates into plasma cells that secrete antibodies. Antibody maturation mainly happens in germinal centers, where activated B cells undergo somatic hypermutation and selection for tighter antigen binding. They can also undergo class-switch recombination to change the antibody isotype, such as from IgM to IgG, IgA, or IgE, without changing antigen specificity."
